---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Status from '../components/Status.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../consts';

const title = 'Work';
const description =
  'Work by Priya Kaur: markAtt, an attendance and homework tracking app for a community Sunday school in Brisbane, and Happy Little Coloring Adventures, an activity book series for children on Amazon.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Work</h1>

  <p class="lead">This is what I have built and published so far.</p>

  <div class="divide">
    <article class="entry">
      <div class="entry__head">
        <h2>markAtt</h2>
        <Status state="in-pilot" />
      </div>

      <p>
        Attendance and homework tracking for a community Sunday school in
        Brisbane, replacing a Google Sheets process. In use with real teachers
        and real students.
      </p>

      <p>
        The interesting part wasn't the screens. It was a seven-table Postgres
        schema with row-level security on every table, then testing that
        security the way I'd test a client's: stale sessions, cross-teacher
        access attempts, offline edits that had to revert cleanly when the
        server rejected them. Attendance uses NULL for "not marked yet" rather
        than defaulting to absent, because a teacher who hasn't opened the roll
        is not the same as a student who didn't show up.
      </p>

      <p>React Native, Expo, Supabase, TypeScript. Currently in pilot.</p>

      <p>
        <a href="/markatt/support/">markAtt support and contact</a>
      </p>

      <!--
        Later: the full markAtt case study goes here, after the pilot term.
        Cover the Google Sheets workflow it replaced, the data model, the
        row-level security policies, and what the pilot actually surfaced.
      -->
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="entry__head">
        <h2>Happy Little Coloring Adventures</h2>
        <Status state="building" />
      </div>

      <p>
        Colouring and word-tracing activity books for children aged 3 to 6,
        self-published through Amazon KDP. Each page pairs a picture to colour
        with a word to trace, so children build early vocabulary and handwriting
        alongside fine motor skills.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href={LINKS.iLoveSpring} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          >I Love Spring</a
        > is the first, with 18 spring-themed pages. Three more in the series are
        in progress.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
